<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Vojislav</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1043</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Vojislav was the ruler of Duklja who threw off Byzantine authority in the 1030s. Around 1042 he defeated a large Byzantine army in the mountains near Bar, securing the independence of Duklja. He founded the Vojislavljević dynasty, under which Duklja later became a kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Contemporary Byzantine writers call Stefan Vojislav a Serb or a Dukljan but give no genealogy; the later and less reliable Chronicle of the Priest of Duklja makes him a cousin of the earlier ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-vladimir">Jovan Vladimir</a>. After the death of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> in 1025, Byzantine power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> weakened, and heavy taxation fed movements for independence. Vojislav held the Byzantine titles of archon and toparch of the Dalmatian forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a>, and was close enough to Katakalon Klazomenites, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of Ragusa, to kidnap him so that he would stand godfather to his son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to John Skylitzes, the Dukljans renounced Byzantine rule around 1034, when Vojislav rebelled after the death of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros">Romanos III Argyros</a>. He was defeated and imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1035 or 1036, and his land was placed under the strategos Theophilos Erotikos. In late 1037 or early 1038 he escaped, returned home and rebelled again. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics he fought off several expeditions and took partial control of Travunija and Zahumlje, so that by 1040 his state ran along the coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a> to his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Skadar</a>, with other courts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebinje">Trebinje</a>, Kotor and Bar.</p><p>In 1039 a ship carrying 10 kentenars of gold to Emperor Michael IV was wrecked off his coast, and Vojislav kept the cargo. The army of General George Probatas sent against him was ambushed in the gorges and routed. In 1042 Emperor Constantine IX sent an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> and bought the support of the župan of Raška, the Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and Prince Ljutovid of Hum. Vojislav and three of his sons attacked it by night in the mountains between Bar and Crmnica, using horns and trumpets to seem more numerous, and routed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vojislav ruled the rest of his years in peace. His widow and five sons, Gojislav, Predimir, Mihailo, Saganek and Radoslav, succeeded him, and he was buried in the Church of St. Andrew at Prapratna, between Bar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>. Under his son Mihailo I, Duklja remained for some decades the centre of the Serbian state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Vojislav” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1040 <b>Stefan Vojislav</b> threw off Byzantine control and defeated a Byzantine army near Bar in 1042.</blockquote><a href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brusno, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.8,19.38&amp;year=1033" aria-label="Brusno on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stefan Vojislav"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4405 5904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4405 5904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.8,19.38&amp;year=1033">What was here in 1033? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
